(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the collections.namedtuple factory in Python. Compare namedtuple with standard tuples, dictionaries, and classes in syntax and memory overhead.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Motivation for namedtuple:) Tj
T*
(   Standard tuples are memory efficient and immutable, but accessing fields via numeric indices) Tj
T*
(   \(t[0], t[1]\) leads to unreadable, brittle code.) Tj
T*
(   Dictionaries provide named fields \(d['x']\), but incur high hash table memory overhead \(~240) Tj
T*
(   bytes\) and are mutable.) Tj
T*
(   collections.namedtuple is a factory function that produces a memory-efficient, immutable tuple) Tj
T*
(   subclass where fields are accessible by both attribute name \(p.x\) and index \(p[0]\)!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Memory & Feature Comparison:) Tj
T*
(   - Standard Tuple: O\(1\) memory \(~48 bytes\), index access only.) Tj
T*
(   - namedtuple: O\(1\) memory \(~48 bytes, exact same as tuple!\), attribute & index access,) Tj
T*
(   immutable.) Tj
T*
(   - Dictionary: High memory overhead \(~240 bytes\), key lookup, mutable.) Tj
T*
(   - Standard Class: High memory overhead due to instance __dict__ \(~152 bytes\) unless using) Tj
T*
(   __slots__.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Python Code Demonstration:) Tj
T*
(   import sys) Tj
T*
(   from collections import namedtuple) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # Define namedtuple type) Tj
T*
(   Point = namedtuple\('Point', ['x', 'y', 'z']\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   p1 = Point\(x=10.5, y=20.0, z=-5.2\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # Access by name and index) Tj
T*
(   print\(f"Point: {p1}"\)) Tj
T*
(   print\(f"Access by Attribute Name: p1.x = {p1.x}, p1.y = {p1.y}"\)) Tj
T*
(   print\(f"Access by Tuple Index: p1[0] = {p1[0]}, p1[1] = {p1[1]}"\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # Conversion to dictionary) Tj
T*
(   p_dict = p1._asdict\(\)) Tj
T*
(   print\(f"Converted to Dict via _asdict\(\): {p_dict}"\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # Memory benchmark) Tj
T*
ET

