12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Implement a Two-Sum Algorithm in Python using Hash Maps \(Dictionaries\) to achieve O\(N\) linear time complexity versus O\(N^2\) brute force.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Problem Context:) Tj
T*
(   Given an array of integers nums and an integer target, return indices of the two numbers such) Tj
T*
(   that they add up to target. Each input has exactly one solution, and you may not use the same) Tj
T*
(   element twice.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Algorithmic Trade-off:) Tj
T*
(   - Brute Force Approach: Nested loops testing every pair \(i, j\).) Tj
T*
(   Time Complexity: O\(N^2\), Space: O\(1\). Unacceptable for N = 100,000.) Tj
T*
(   - Hash Map Approach \(One-Pass\):) Tj
T*
(   As we iterate through the list with index i and value num:) Tj
T*
(   Calculate complement: complement = target - num.) Tj
T*
(   If complement exists in seen_map: return \(seen_map[complement], i\).) Tj
T*
(   Else: insert seen_map[num] = i.) Tj
T*
(   Time Complexity: O\(N\), Space: O\(N\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Python Implementation:) Tj
T*
(   def two_sum_hash_map\(nums: list, target: int\) -> tuple:) Tj
T*
(   seen = {} # Maps number_value -> index) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   for idx, num in enumerate\(nums\):) Tj
T*
(   complement = target - num) Tj
T*
(   if complement in seen:) Tj
T*
(   return \(seen[complement], idx\)) Tj
T*
(   seen[num] = idx) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   return \(\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # Verification) Tj
T*
(   dataset = [2, 7, 11, 15]) Tj
T*
(   target_val = 9) Tj
T*
(   result_indices = two_sum_hash_map\(dataset, target_val\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   print\(f"Array: {dataset} | Target: {target_val}"\)) Tj
T*
(   print\(f"Solution Indices: {result_indices}"\)) Tj
T*
(   print\(f"Verification: {dataset[result_indices[0]]} + {dataset[result_indices[1]]} =) Tj
T*
(   {target_val}"\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Memory Model Trace:) Tj
T*
ET

